  <data name="Story_Badge" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Why Famnances exists</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Story_Body1" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>When a family moves to a new country, something curious happens with money: the numbers change currency, but the conversations about the budget stay in the language of home. You still talk about "groceries" or "savings for school" in Spanish, even if the bank statement arrives in English.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Story_Body2" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>That mismatch is exactly the problem Famnances set out to solve — not just for accessibility's sake, but because a real budgeting tool has to respect that the language of money and the language of the heart don't always match.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Story_Body3" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>That's why the app is built around the period — the unit of time a family actually thinks about its money in — and the household, not the individual user, as the unit that shares that budget, rather than the bank account.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Story_Quote" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>It started as a way to organize my own household's accounts — between two languages and two countries. If it works the same way for you, that's exactly what it's here for.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Story_QuoteAuthor" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>— Sebastián, creator of Famnances</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Story_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A family budget shouldn't have to live across three different apps.</value>
  </data>
